================================================================================
Locating the exact flaws in the 2024 Journal of Cryptology proof (pp. 20-25)
================================================================================
  Flaw 1 (Section 4.4, p. 24, BadSamp & eps_ratio):
    In Section 4.4 (p. 24), for a good transcript where {j : X_i^-[j] is fresh}
    is non-empty with smallest index j_i < l_i, the proof samples MM_i^-[j_i]
    randomly and claims that Pr[BadSamp] = Pr[MM_i^-[l_i] not fresh] <= 1/(2^n-2*sigma)
    'due to the randomness of MM_i^-[j_i]', and then samples CC_i^-[l_i+1] uniformly.
    In reality, during COLM_0.dec (Fig. 3, lines 6-18), MM_i^-[l_i] = E_K^-1(X_i^-[l_i])
    is determined solely by C_i^-[l_i] and W_i^-[l_i-1]; it does NOT depend on MM_i^-[j_i]!
    When D[129] = 0 (Fade), W_i^-[l_i-1] = W_1^+[l_i-1] and C_i^-[l_i] = C_1^+[l_i], so
    X_i^-[l_i] == X_1^+[l_i], MM_i^-[l_i] == MM_1^+[l_i], and CC_i^-[l_i+1] == CC_1^+[l_i+1]
    hold deterministically with probability 1 regardless of MM_i^-[j_i].

  Flaw 2 (Lemma 1, p. 20 & Bad Event B6, pp. 22-23):
    In Transplant (l = 129), every block of C_i^- comes from either Query 1 or Query 2,
    so no CC_i^-[j] is fresh. Bad Event B6 (p. 23) invokes Lemma 1 (p. 20) to claim that
    L_X(W[0], Y[1..j+1]) == X'[j+1] forces W[0] == W'[0], overlooking that when two
    queries share a 129-block plaintext prefix (X_1^+[r] == X_2^+[r]) and j+1 >= 129,
    the GF(2^128) relation 3 * sum_{r in {1,3,4,5,6,7}} 2^(r-1) = 2^128 mod f(x)
    transfers W_i^-[128] onto W_2^+[128] even though W_1^+[0] != W_2^+[0]!

  2024 J. Cryptol. sigma = a + 2m + 3(q+s) = 795 at a=6, m=390, q=2, s=1   OK
  2024 J. Cryptol. Theorem 2 bound at sigma=795 is 2^-105.92               OK
  2016 Addendum sigma_auth = 398 and Theorem 2 bound is 2^-107.56          OK

